WebArchiver - handoff notes
===========================

These notes carry the work over to a new conversation. The owner's general
rules (tech rules, C subset, coding style, privacy, values) are already
known there and are not repeated here.


1. What the owner wants
-----------------------

A small program that archives whole websites for offline use and keeps
them up to date, with version history. In detail:

Downloading
- Input: a text file with one link per line (blank lines and text after
  "#" ignored). A link without a scheme is tried over https first and
  falls back to plain http if https does not answer (privacy was chosen
  over the "no HTTPS" rule here, by the owner).
- Each domain gets its own directory, named after the domain (lower case,
  ":port" becomes "_port"). Without an archive directory argument the
  domain directories go into the current directory; with one, into it.
  Several links on one domain share its directory.
- Giving only a directory updates the whole archive (every link ever
  archived there is remembered in .archive-sites.txt).
- Up to 10 domains download at the same time; links of one domain run one
  after another.
- Request pace is automatic: 0.2 s between requests; if a site pushes back
  (HTTP 429, 503, or requests needing retries) it drops to 1 s, then 3 s,
  and stays slow for 7 days. A finer ladder (0.1, 0.2, ...) was rejected
  on purpose: repeatedly hitting a site's limit risks IP bans.

Storage
- Every file stored once. Big files (video, audio, archives, disk images,
  PDF) are identified before downloading (same URL after redirects, same
  ETag + size, or same size + first 1 MiB) so a file reachable under
  several paths/symlinks is downloaded once. Other files are compared by
  SHA-256 after download. Duplicates are deleted; no symlinks anywhere;
  links are pointed at the kept copy.
- Every link and CSS path is rewritten to a relative path, so a site
  directory works from any location. Links like "/css/x.css" and
  "http://site/page" must become relative. Links to pages that were not
  archived point to the live site. CSS/JS/images/fonts that wget missed
  (e.g. on a CDN) are fetched afterwards into _external/<host>/.
- Each domain directory is self-contained (duplicates are removed within
  a domain; a big file already downloaded for another domain is copied,
  not downloaded again).

Safety
- Stopping is safe at any time: Ctrl+C, network loss, crash, power cut.
  Ctrl+C stops the downloads, then still fixes links for what was
  downloaded; a second Ctrl+C quits at once. The next run repairs
  half-written files and continues without downloading finished files
  again. No wget process may be left running after a stop.

Updates and versions
- An update re-fetches every page (HTML/CSS) and compares it, so a change
  on any page is found even if the home page did not change. Other files
  are downloaded only if they changed.
- The domain directory is always the current version. When an update
  replaces a file, only the old copy is kept, once, in .archive-history/
  (content-addressed). Any old version can be rebuilt as a normal
  directory DOMAIN@VERSION/. The owner did not want index.v2.html style
  copies next to the current files.

Progress display (terminal)
- One header line with counts, then one line per domain: name, step,
  amount downloaded, files downloaded this run, speed in files per minute
  (one unit only - the owner asked for a single, simple figure), elapsed
  time, errors, and the current URL or status. Colours on a terminal,
  plain text otherwise.
- Steps: downloading / resuming / updating (with the pace, e.g. "@0.2s"),
  big files (with file name and percentage), linking, ready (new or
  resumed site) / updated (existing site), partial (stopped early),
  failed (with the log to read). Queued sites say whether they are new,
  unfinished or will be updated. The final status of a site shows what
  changed and the site's total size in the archive, so an update that
  downloaded only 2 files is clearly not a site with only 2 files.
- Each site is linked and its version saved as soon as its own download
  finishes, while others keep downloading.

Pages (no JavaScript, XHTML 1.0 Transitional, CSS1/CSS2)
- archive.html in the archive directory: every site with status, files,
  size, number of versions, last saved time, links to the site, its
  history and the live original; totals at the top. Relative links only.
- history.html: every version of every site with the changed files.
- --serve: a tiny local web server (127.0.0.1 only, one process, no
  threads) showing the same page plus a form to add links, a button to
  update the whole archive, a progress page and "Rebuild" buttons for
  old versions.

Privacy
- A common browser User-Agent instead of a program name; times in UTC;
  no absolute paths (which contain the user name) in pages; nothing is
  sent anywhere except wget's requests to the listed sites; no telemetry.
- Tor: optional and not wanted for now ("torsocks webarchiver ..." works).

Other decisions by the owner
- Name: WebArchiver (one word), command: webarchiver.
- No license for now.
- Plain C, suckless-like: readable first, minimal second. Functionality
  and look must stay as they are.
- Must run on 64-bit; the Eee PC reference means "be efficient", not
  "the owner uses a 32-bit machine". The code is portable to 32-bit too.
- The owner is not a programmer yet: explain technical choices simply.
- The owner will start with a fresh archive, so no converter for data of
  the earlier script versions is needed (it was removed).


2. What was done
----------------

History: it started as a bash script around wget, grew an embedded Python
helper (dedupe, links, versions, pages, server) and was then rewritten in
plain C89 (+ long long) as WebArchiver, keeping behaviour and look.

The program still uses wget for every network access (pages, probing big
files with "wget -S --spider", partial reads with a Range header, and
resumable downloads with "wget --continue"). It has no network or TLS code
of its own. Allowed by the owner's scope rule: wget is a tool.

Files
  main.c     command line, settings, preparing each domain (repair after a
             crash, choosing new/resume/update, snapshot), the parallel
             loop (fork per domain), progress table, Ctrl+C handling
  fetch.c    running wget: worker per domain, https->http choice, pace and
             push-back detection, probing and downloading big files
  finish.c   after wget: repair of interrupted runs, duplicate removal,
             big files, link rewriting (byte-level scanners for
             attributes, srcset, url(), @import, URLs in scripts),
             versions, everything run per domain as "finalize"
  store.c    data files: state, url map, indexes, hash cache, versions,
             history objects
  page.c     archive.html, history.html, --versions, --restore
  serve.c    --serve (select() loop, forms, static files; hidden
             .archive-* files are not served)
  url.c      URL join/split (RFC 3986 style), percent coding, paths
  util.c     buffers, string lists, hash table, files, time (UTC), sort
  sha256.c   SHA-256
  config.def.h  defaults (copied to config.h by make)

Archive directory layout
  <domain>/              current version of each site
  <domain>@<id>/         rebuilt old versions
  archive.html, history.html
  .archive-sites.txt     all links of the archive
  .archive-logs/<d>.log          wget log (appended, read for url->file)
  .archive-logs/<d>.linking.log  finishing log of the last run
  .archive-state/<d>.state       complete, phase (idle/crawl/finalize),
                                 mode, log_offset
  .archive-state/<d>.pre         snapshot "size mtime" before wget ran
  .archive-state/<d>.urlmap      url -> file
  .archive-state/<d>.index       url:/sha:/etag:/head: keys -> file,
                                 dup:<file> -> kept copy
  .archive-state/<d>.hashes      file -> "sha size mtime"
  .archive-state/<d>.base/.wait/.slow/.status/.rc  small run files
  .archive-state/global.index    big-file keys across domains (mkdir
                                 used as a lock)
  .archive-state/STOP            exists while stopping
  .archive-history/<d>/<id>.files    file -> "sha size" of a version
  .archive-history/<d>/<id>.changes  "+/~/-<TAB>file"
  .archive-history/<d>/<id>.info     partial 0/1
  .archive-history/objects/xx/<sha>  old copies, stored once
  .archive-lock/pid              one run per archive at a time
All own data is flat text, "key<TAB>value" per line.

How the safety works
- Before wget runs, a snapshot of every file's size and time is saved and
  the phase becomes "crawl". wget runs with --no-clobber (new/resume) or
  --timestamping --no-if-modified-since --backups=1 (update; the old copy
  becomes name.1). After a stop or crash, files that changed since the
  snapshot but are not logged as finished by wget are deleted (or their
  .1 backup is put back). Backups needed by old versions move into
  history objects. Page mtimes are kept when links are rewritten, so
  wget's update check stays correct; on update all page mtimes are set to
  0 so every page is fetched again.

Testing done (all against local test servers, on 64-bit x86-64)
- duplicates, symlinked videos, root-relative and CDN links, srcset,
  CSS url() and fonts, malformed links, windows-1251 file names
- Ctrl+C (no wget left), resume, kill -9 during an update and recovery
- update, versions, restore giving back the exact original files
- pace stepping 0.2 -> 1 -> 3 s against a rate-limiting server
- https -> http fallback; big-file progress and continued download
- --serve form, refresh while running, Rebuild button, one thread
- generated pages are well-formed XHTML; build has no warnings with
  -std=c89 -pedantic -Wno-long-long -Wall -Wextra, static binary; no
  line over 79 columns; ASCII only

Library functions used beyond the owner's allowed list: opendir/readdir/
closedir, sigaction, socket/bind/listen/accept/select/setsockopt, ioctl,
utimensat, readlink, pipe, dup2, fork/waitpid/kill, getcwd, fsync. All are
system-call wrappers; the owner accepted keeping them because replacing
them would fit the rules worse. htons/htonl, isatty and signal() were
replaced.


3. Open points
--------------

- The C version has not yet been run against real websites. The earlier
  script version was, on the owner's list of sites, which found real
  problems (sites that only answer plain http, pages in windows-1251,
  malformed links); all of them are handled and tested locally now.
- Pages removed from a site stay in the current version (wget never
  deletes files).
- Links that scripts build at run time are not rewritten.
- An update re-checks every file with a HEAD request at the site's pace;
  large sites take a while to update even when little changed.
- Duplicate links rewritten in pages that did not change on the server can
  point at a copy that later changed (rare; fixed by the next update of
  that page).
- No license chosen yet.
- Possible later work: shrinking the code, an optional Tor setting.


4. Added to the dotfiles
------------------------

The program now lives in dotfiles/webarchiver/ and install.sh builds it
like the suckless tools: "./install.sh webarchiver" installs it into
~/.system/bin, no root needed. config.h is kept in the repo (as for dwm).
Changes made for the newer version of the owner's rules:

- Build: x86-64 is the default target (the machine it is built on); the
  Eee PC stays a yardstick for speed and memory only. Linked static and
  stripped (-s), so the binary carries no symbol names or build paths.
- Privacy: wget is given --hsts-file inside .archive-state, so it no
  longer writes ~/.wget-hsts (a list of every https site visited, kept
  outside the archive). Each site's wget log is started fresh when a new
  run begins after a finished one (the url map already holds what it
  found), and web-run.log keeps only the newest --serve run, so logs
  hold only what is needed and do not grow forever.
- Efficiency: the push-back check (every 2 s per downloading site) read
  the whole wget log each time; it now reads only the lines added since
  the last check.
- Keyboard: the --serve page has access keys (a: links box, u: update
  everything, h: all versions, p: progress) and says so on the page.
- Code was already 4-space indented, no tabs, under 80 columns.
